RSpice conformance corpus
=========================

This tree is the ngspice-46 test corpus, vendored as RSpice's conformance
oracle suite. The
.out files are recorded ngspice-46 results and serve as the golden
references.

Local normalization: `bsim3soifd/nmosfd.mod` repairs the malformed model-card
token run `RTH0 94 = .006` to the intended `RTH0 = .006`, matching the sibling
BSIM3SOI model cards. The recorded ngspice-46 `.out` references remain
unchanged.

How RSpice runs it: crates/rspice-core/tests/ngspice_regression.rs drives
the decks suite by suite (test_ngspice_<suite>_suite). Per-deck validation
contracts live in validation-manifest.tsv (smoke, scripted_control,
expected_unsupported, expected_unsupported_without_veriloga_builtins,
expected_unsolvable, reference_unsolvable, locked_grid, measures); measure
gates are declared in <deck>.gates.tsv sidecars next to their decks. The
full run gates nightly CI against the conformance watermark
(.github/workflows/nightly.yml).

Upstream runner/build files that RSpice does not execute - `bin/`,
`Makefile.am`, CMC `qaSpec`/`parameters`/`reference` harness trees, and
`ChangeLog` - are intentionally trimmed from this vendored corpus. The retained
files are the RSpice-discovered `.cir` decks, checked-in `.out` oracles,
required include/model data, measure sidecars, this README, and the validation
manifest.

Layer map - separate validation surfaces:
  tests/ngspice/  conformance against recorded ngspice-46 oracles (this tree)
  tests/xyce/     vendored Xyce Regression Suite runtime corpus
  benchmarks/     performance rig decks + published scoreboards

The upstream ngspice README excerpt below is retained for provenance; its
`configure`/`Makefile.am`/`make check` instructions apply to upstream ngspice,
not RSpice.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

ngspice test and example files:
===============================

This directory is organized as a tree of subdirectories containing test files
for devices and analyses implemented in ngspice.

Some files comes from the original Spice3f5 package and others have been 
contributed by developers and users.


File Extension Convention:

.cir : Circuit file. This can be a simple circuit description or a spice2
       input file.
.out : .cir files have been run and results are recorded into this
       type of file. This is useful if want to test ngspice against
       known (hopefully correct results).        
               
REPLICATE TESTS

To replicate tests you have to launch configure without any option and
compile ngspice. In the future this will change.

TO ADD NEW TESTS

Take an existing test and adopt it to your liking.  Add the test
script and its supporting files to Makefile.am.  Use `make check' to
see your new test in action.
